# Horizontal scaling for the bot runner.
#
# The 2026-08-19 pilot ran on one t3.medium with min 1 / max 1. That single
# setting was the ceiling: five concurrent one-participant sessions held a 500ms
# median response at 64% CPU, and eight collapsed — replies fell from 261 to 23
# while the processor sat at only 77%, because work queued behind speech
# recognition and was abandoned. No code change raises that; nothing could be
# added under load.
#
# Sizing comes from measurement, not preference:
#   1 x 3-participant session   23.6% avg / 32.1% peak of 2 vCPU
#   5 x 1-participant sessions  64% avg of 2 vCPU
#   => roughly 0.25 vCPU per session, so 4 vCPU carries ~10 comfortably
#
# c6i.xlarge rather than a larger t3: t-family instances are burstable and
# throttle to a fraction of baseline after hours of sustained load, which is
# precisely the shape of a pilot day. c6i has no such cliff.
#
# Bots run in whichever instance served their /start, so load distributes without
# a dispatcher. The one shared background job — reconcile_stale_conversations —
# is idempotent, so extra instances duplicate effort rather than corrupt state.
#
# Scale-up trigger is deliberately below the point where quality degrades. The
# tail is the early warning: p99 response nearly doubled between three and five
# sessions (2,754ms -> 5,076ms) while the median stayed flat at ~500ms. Waiting
# for high average CPU means waiting until users already feel it.
#
# Known limitation of running more than one instance. audio_tracks keeps its
# per-speaker WAV sinks in a per-process registry, so start_recording_for_room
# only finds a sink if it runs on the instance hosting that bot. The common path
# is safe: auto_record fires from inside the bot itself, in-process. But a manual
# "start recording" from the console goes through the load balancer and may land
# elsewhere, in which case composite recording still works and per-speaker WAVs
# silently do not. Fixing it properly means the sink registry stops being
# in-process; until then, prefer auto_record.
#
# meet is deliberately NOT scaled out — see meet/.ebextensions/scaling.config.
# tests/test_scaling_config.py guards both.
option_settings:
  aws:autoscaling:launchconfiguration:
    InstanceType: c6i.xlarge

  aws:autoscaling:asg:
    MinSize: 2
    MaxSize: 6

  # Average CPU across the group. Up at 55%, down at 25% — a wide gap so a
  # single talkative meeting does not cause the group to flap.
  aws:autoscaling:trigger:
    MeasureName: CPUUtilization
    Statistic: Average
    Unit: Percent
    Period: 1
    EvaluationPeriods: 1
    UpperThreshold: 55
    UpperBreachScaleIncrement: 2
    LowerThreshold: 25
    LowerBreachScaleIncrement: -1

  # A voice session is a live conversation. Draining has to outlast one, or a
  # scale-in event cuts people off mid-sentence.
  aws:elasticbeanstalk:command:
    DeploymentPolicy: Rolling
    BatchSizeType: Fixed
    BatchSize: 1
    Timeout: 1800

  aws:elbv2:loadbalancer:
    IdleTimeout: 3600
